scottish first minister nicola sturgeon  says updated guidance about christmas  will be issued  later today and the welsh first minister  mark drakeford has been explaining why  he's concluded  that a smaller christmas is a safer  christmas  the advice of the welsh government is  that only two households should come  together  to form an exclusive christmas bubble  during  the five-day period the fewer people we  mix with in our own homes  the less chance we have of catching or  spreading  the virus nobody wants to be ill  at christmas and none of us  want to give this deadly disease  to our closest family or friends  we don't intend to take away the  flexibility  that in my view wouldn't be fair at this  stage  and it wouldn't be realistic either and  indeed it may risk undermining rather  than strengthening compliance with the  overall  covert guidance however we  and scottish government do intend to  strengthen the guidance we give to  people about whether and how they should  make  use of the flexibility the guidance in  scotland is already a bit tighter  than elsewhere but we hope we can come  to a four nations agreement on a clear  and united message to the public  in a moment we'll speak to james  matthews in edinburgh first let's go to  westminster and our political  correspondent  tom rayner tom the prime minister said  there was four nations agreement  on this it doesn't really feel like it  at the moment does it  well certainly from what boris johnson  said at prime minister's questions  you're right it was  uh conveyed as though the result of  talks that have been going on chaired by  michael gove  with the devolved administrations that  started yesterday and concluded this  morning  had arrived at a position uh where they  had unanimous  unanimously agreed not to change the law  when it comes to the christmas  regulations now of course  that is the case it seems there has been  agreement not to change  the letter of the law but what we are  seeing from  what you heard there from mark drakeford  and indeed nicholas sturgeon is that the  spirit of the law if you like  uh does appear to be an area uh where  the four parts of the uk  are diverging somewhat mark drapeford  saying that he wants to see  households of two be the main bubble  in terms of the relaxations over  christmas period rather than three now  that's guidance it's advice it means  that if you're in wales  and you meet in a group of three  households you are not breaking  the law but what you are doing is going  against the advice of the welsh  government  and clearly this distinction is  important because  uh the law is set in westminster but  clearly the devolved administrations  have powers when it comes to public  health advice and public health  guidance uh to operate in their own way  and clearly mark drakeford is deciding  uh that he will do that now we know that  the prime minister is going to hold a  news conference at some point  this afternoon the time yet to be  confirmed alongside the  chief medical officer chris whittie and  so there will be big questions for boris  johnson  about whether or not there will be  changes to the government advice with  regards to  england even if the law itself doesn't  change and certainly from the tone boris  johnson struck  sarah in prime minister's questions it  would seem that it  doesn't look likely that boris johnson  will be particularly  uh inclined towards changing the  guidance specifically  uh but perhaps what we will see is the  messaging uh  intensified uh lots of suggestions we  heard it from robert jenwick  uh the community secretary on sky news  this morning about people taking uh  personal responsibility using their  judgment  is it really necessary to use the full  five days is it really necessary  to see an elderly relative could you  wait till easter  that kind of persuasive attempt to make  people take responsibility  may be the direction that the government  takes with regards to england but  clearly  it is already at odds with the much more  straightforward and sober guidance that  the welsh government and the scottish  government  seem to be prepared to put out tom  westminster thank you to edinburgh  and james the guidance from the scottish  government  also tougher than the law  the guidance up here is uh broadly  similar  in terms of the principles they are on a  similar track to the rest of the united  kingdom  but there is that flexibility uh sarah  yes slightly tougher  uh i'm being told that it will not  change  in terms of the substance nicola  sturgeon at our covert briefing was  speaking about  strength and guidance the in practical  terms  i don't expect that to change it will be  you know eight members three households  five days over christmas  the guidance that will be underpinned in  due course will be luke  think hard about whether or not you need  to be together and don't regard  these maximums as targets so don't  regard five days  as five days you should spend together  she said try and spend  one together try and avoid overnight  stays in terms of  interaction make that outdoors if at all  possible and don't regard eight people  being together  as your target try and uh get somewhere  far less than that so  i expect we'll hear possibly over the  course of today  uh that that guidance will be  strengthened sarah but like i say  i don't expect it to change not today at  any rate nicola sturgeon  did leave a get out she was asked well  could it change between now  and the christmas period and she made it  clear that there was  that possibility should surrounding  circumstances be any different  in due course james in edinburgh thank  you
 

